 "e": 170.14}, {"w": "climb", "s": 170.14, "e": 170.54}, {"w": "with", "s": 170.54, "e": 170.76}, {"w": "an", "s": 170.76, "e": 170.96}, {"w": "average", "s": 170.96, "e": 171.24}, {"w": "gradient", "s": 171.24, "e": 171.52}, {"w": "of", "s": 171.52, "e": 171.7}, {"w": "8", "s": 171.7, "e": 172.62}, {"w": "Chapeau", "s": 172.62, "e": 173.2}], "segments": [{"s": 0.0, "e": 4.52, "text": "Gears are a fundamental part of a bicycle. They allow you to take on steep climbs and keep the"}, {"s": 4.52, "e": 8.92, "text": "pedals turning on the flats all at the same time. But how do they actually work? The gears on a bike"}, {"s": 8.92, "e": 13.5, "text": "can be broken down into three areas of componentry. The chainrings, including the front derailleur,"}, {"s": 13.9, "e": 18.18, "text": "the cassette, including the rear derailleur and pulley wheels, and the shifters. The chainrings"}, {"s": 18.18, "e": 22.6, "text": "are attached to the cranks and most road bikes have two of them. Chainrings are defined by the"}, {"s": 22.6, "e": 26.7, "text": "number of teeth on the inner and outer rings and these days setups for road bikes can be grouped"}, {"s": 26.7, "e": 33.02, "text": "into three categories. Standard, semi-compact and compact. A standard chain set has 53 teeth on the"}, {"s": 33.02, "e": 39.14, "text": "big ring and 39 teeth on the inner. A semi-compact has 52 teeth on the outer ring and 36 on the inner"}, {"s": 39.14, "e": 44.26, "text": "ring and a compact has 50 teeth on the big ring and 34 on the inner ring. A standard chain set"}, {"s": 44.26, "e": 48.56, "text": "has the hardest gears of the three setups while a compact has the easiest. The group of cogs"}, {"s": 48.56, "e": 53.32, "text": "attached to the rear wheel is the cassette. Most road bikes will have between 9 and 12 cogs ranging"}, {"s": 53.32, "e": 58.14, "text": "from 11 teeth on the smallest cog up to 32 teeth on the biggest. The number of cogs determines the"}, {"s": 58.14, "e": 62.96, "text": "so-called speed of the bike. Nine cogs means a nine-speed bike and so on. The derailleurs are"}, {"s": 62.96, "e": 67.06, "text": "operated by the shifters on the handlebars. The derailleurs shift the chain between the inner and"}, {"s": 67.06, "e": 71.4, "text": "outer ring on the front and the cogs on the cassette on the back. The front derailleur is operated by"}, {"s": 71.4, "e": 75.64, "text": "the left shifter and the rear derailleur by the right. So that's a bike's gears in a nutshell,"}, {"s": 75.86, "e": 83.3, "text": "but what about gear ratios? A gear ratio is expressed simply as the number of teeth on the"}, {"s": 83.3, "e": 87.76, "text": "chain is currently sitting in. This then determines the number of rotations your rear wheel will make"}, {"s": 87.76, "e": 92.88, "text": "for one complete pedal stroke. For example, if you are in the big ring on the front, let's say it's a 52,"}, {"s": 93.06, "e": 98.42, "text": "and the smallest cog on the back, let's say a 12, then the ratio is 52 to 12. Then you just divide the"}, {"s": 98.42, "e": 103.56, "text": "big ring number by the small ring, so 52 divided by 12, to give you the number of rotations. In our"}, {"s": 103.56, "e": 108.9, "text": "example, 4.3. So one turn of the pedals will result in 4.3 rotations of the wheel. That would be our"}, {"s": 109.06, "e": 113.9, "text": "gear, of course, with each pedal stroke resulting in the greatest distance travelled. The reverse is"}, {"s": 113.9, "e": 118.96, "text": "true in relation to the smallest gear. With a chain in the small ring, a 36 for example, on the front,"}, {"s": 119.28, "e": 124.78, "text": "and the biggest cog on the back, say a 28, the result would be 36 divided by 28, giving us just"}, {"s": 124.78, "e": 130.02, "text": "1.2 rotations of the wheel for one pedal stroke. This gives us the least distance but is ideal for"}, {"s": 130.02, "e": 133.6, "text": "climbing to help keep your cadence as high as possible. The moral of the story is obviously"}, {"s": 133.6, "e": 137.34, "text": "to get your gearing right next time you're in the market for a new bike. If you love climbing,"}, {"s": 137.34, "e": 141.96, "text": "then a compact or semi-compact setup will be best, while for racers and rulers, a standard"}, {"s": 141.96, "e": 146.54, "text": "might be more appropriate. It's a question of your preferred cadence as well. Low cadence riders will"}, {"s": 146.54, "e": 150.96, "text": "prefer more traditional setups, while high cadence riders generally prefer more modern compacts."}, {"s": 151.08, "e": 155.2, "text": "Having said that, Lance Armstrong was known as a high cadence rider, he generally liked to ride"}, {"s": 155.2, "e": 160.64, "text": "above 90 rpm, and he used a 22 teeth cassette on his standard setup for the hill climb on"}, {"s": 160.64, "e": 165.58, "text": "stage 11 at the 2001 Tour. He had used a 23 on the previous day's climb up Alpe d'Huez,"}, {"s": 165.58, "e": 169.24, "text": "and rumour has it, he felt that was too low. Imagine that,"}, {"s": 169.26, "e": 173.2, "text": "up a 13km climb with an average gradient of 8%. Chapeau."}]}}